  prose(<<~'CANONICAL_PROSE', origin: :published)
    ## Mechanism
    
    A collective pressure-rupture technique in which an orca pod synchronizes blood-magic to create localized crushing force at range. The water surrounding the target remains undisturbed; the force manifests *within* the target's body, collapsing bone and organ from the inside. Evidence recovered from victims at Aurora Stack's eastern settlement confirms massive internal hemorrhaging with no external trauma — penguins fell mid-stride with their internal chambers simply compressed.
    
    Khrrak's Collapse subsumes older practices from the Grend Blood-Arts and derives its core principle from the individual technique known as Gut Inversion, scaling that inward-directed force through coordinated pod intent. Where the Rupture-Song projects concussive pressure outward through a single practitioner, the Collapse inverts the same principle collectively and at distance — a distinction with significant implications for defensive response.
    
    ## Practitioners and Cost
    
    Practitioners bleed into synchronized patterns along pre-scarified channels, locking the pod's intent into a single compression event. The technique demands perfect coordination: a pod that breaks rhythm does not merely fail — the force rebounds, killing the practitioners themselves.
    
    Gnarkzund Zaith, the orca raider who died at Aurora Stack during the Incursion, refined the technique into a tactical weapon and first deployed it against the Stack's eastern settlement during the third wave. His associate Thung Thrakrek, the war-leader scarred from raids on ☽'scar Ramtatus~, also practiced the art; Thung Thrakrek's left eye went milky after the Shelf campaign. He will not say whether the magic took it.
    
    The pressure-marks scarified into practitioners carry a dangerous reciprocity: external force applied to the marks can trigger the technique's inward collapse against the bearer. Gnarkzund Zaith's marks detonated under forge-fire heat from the ☽'micseleia' Dagger during fighting in ☽'slew Ravium~.
    
    ## Status
    
    Rarely taught. Too many apprentices died holding the pattern. Successful practitioners report cognitive fog lasting days. The technique is not forbidden — it is simply too expensive to learn.
  CANONICAL_PROSE
  annotation "note_1771585864136_0", anchor: "derives its core principle from the individual technique known as Gut Inversion", text: "Read what this says. Gut Inversion is a penguin counter-technique — developed at Aurora Stack by watching their own die, then taught widely as a defensive art. It tears open orca pressure-bonds from inside. The orca watched this happen to their own raiders, understood the principle better than the penguins who wielded it, and scaled it into a collective killing technique aimed back at penguins. The defenders taught their enemy the geometry of inward-directed force by demonstrating it in self-defense. I wrote both entries. I did not connect them in the main text because the implication is unbearable, and I am apparently still capable of cowardice even when I have already confessed to it once.", type: :commentary, display: :full
  annotation "note_1771585864136_1", anchor: "first deployed it against the Stack's eastern settlement during the third wave", text: "'Third wave' is Qingqe'frostlings field terminology for orca assault phases. Orca do not organize raids into numbered waves. They use pressure-pulse sequencing keyed to tidal rhythm. I flagged this exact translation problem in the Thung Thrakrek entry and then repeated the error here. Inexcusable.", type: :pedantic, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771585864136_2", anchor: "Gnarkzund Zaith's marks detonated under forge-fire heat from the ☽'micseleia' Dagger during fighting in ☽'slew Ravium~", text: "Which I already established preceded the fighting at Aurora Stack. So his scarification channels ruptured at Ravium, he survived that, and then died at the Stack under the Grulkzul Omega Strike. A practitioner whose own pressure-marks had already been blown open continued fighting through at least one more major engagement. Either the marks were less catastrophically detonated than 'detonated' implies, or Zaith's pain tolerance belonged to a different order of creature than the one I am comfortable describing.", type: :correction, display: :full
